1
00:00:02,660 --> 00:00:06,900
Proposition one asks whether California should borrow money for

2
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:07,300
housing.

3
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:10,940
A bond supplies money now, while repayments and interest

4
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:12,500
stretch across future budgets.

5
00:00:13,420 --> 00:00:17,560
Some affordable housing already receives public grants or low-cost

6
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:17,940
loans.

7
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:23,320
Developers combine that assistance with other financing, usually in

8
00:00:23,320 --> 00:00:26,660
exchange for keeping homes affordable to eligible households.

9
00:00:26,660 --> 00:00:31,730
This measure authorizes eleven point two five billion dollars:

10
00:00:31,730 --> 00:00:34,505
ten billion for housing programs, and one point two

11
00:00:34,505 --> 00:00:37,120
five billion for veterans’ home loans.

12
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:41,020
Those two portions have different repayment sources.

13
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:44,420
Imagine a renter looking for an affordable apartment.

14
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:47,900
An apartment project might have land and a builder

15
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:51,720
but still need cheaper financing to offer below-market rents.

16
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:54,440
A public loan can help close that gap.

17
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:58,500
This illustrates the mechanism, not a guarantee of a

18
00:00:58,500 --> 00:00:59,160
particular rent.

19
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:02,480
A housing counselor explains the available programs.

20
00:01:03,340 --> 00:01:06,060
The largest allocation is seven point two billion dollars

21
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:10,980
to acquire, build, or renovate multifamily rental housing.

22
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:15,800
Projects generally reserve units for lower-income households for fifty-five

23
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:16,440
years.

24
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:22,660
Other allocations support homeownership, infrastructure, farmworker housing, student housing,

25
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:23,900
and tribal housing.

26
00:01:24,580 --> 00:01:28,400
Eligibility depends on the particular program; passing the bond

27
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:30,660
does not give every applicant a home.

28
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:34,360
The analyst estimates subsidies for up to forty thousand

29
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:35,560
multifamily units.

30
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:39,200
That includes housing acquired or renovated, so it is

31
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:42,860
not a promise of forty thousand additional newly built

32
00:01:42,860 --> 00:01:43,220
homes.

33
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:46,740
The General Fund would repay the housing portion at

34
00:01:46,740 --> 00:01:49,740
about five hundred to six hundred million dollars a

35
00:01:49,740 --> 00:01:52,020
year for roughly twenty-five years.

36
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:56,020
Veterans’ loan payments would repay their separate bond portion.

37
00:01:56,560 --> 00:02:00,620
A voter might support financing affordable homes sooner rather

38
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:03,500
than waiting for enough money in annual budgets.

39
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:07,740
Supporting construction and preserving restricted rents can help specific

40
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:11,920
households even when the broader market remains expensive.

41
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:15,340
You might ask whether the long commitment is worthwhile.

42
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:19,220
A skeptical voter could prefer paying as funds become

43
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:23,740
available, or addressing permitting and construction costs first.

44
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:28,280
Interest adds expense, and future repayments compete with other

45
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:28,760
services.

46
00:02:29,420 --> 00:02:32,500
Money alone does not remove every obstacle to building.

47
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:36,420
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 55

48
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:40,840
percent said yes, 43 percent no, and 2 percent

49
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:41,480
were unsure.

50
00:02:42,220 --> 00:02:45,280
These are dated survey results, not votes already cast.

51
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:50,160
Yes authorizes this borrowing; no rejects this bond, while

52
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:52,360
existing housing programs continue.

53
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:55,860
Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links,

54
00:02:55,860 --> 00:02:57,620
and official voter guide.

55
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:02,140
Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice.
